# Per-file git commits

When the user asks to commit (or commit and push), **create one commit per file**. Do not stage several files and apply one message to all of them.

## Default

- Commit only after the user asks. This rule changes *how* commits are split, not *when*.
- For each included path, `git add` that path and `git commit` it alone.
- Write a concise message for **that file**, focused on why it changed.
- A rename/move is one commit: stage the old path and the new path together.
- Unrelated untracked junk (`_tmp_*`, caches, samples the user did not ask to include) stays unstaged.
- Never add Cursor / agent `Co-authored-by` trailers (see `no-cursor-coauthor.mdc`).

```text
# ❌ BAD — one bulk commit
git add mctoolkit/ui/plot.py tests/test_plot.py
git commit -m "Fix plot hover and tests"

# ✅ GOOD — one commit per file
git add mctoolkit/ui/plot.py
git commit -m "Keep plot hover hits aligned after WebEngine resize."
git add tests/test_plot.py
git commit -m "Cover plot hover alignment after WebEngine resize."
```

## Exception

Combine files into a single commit **only** when the user explicitly says to (e.g. “one commit”, “commit these together”, “bulk commit”). If they do not say that, split.
